  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 158 PM CDT Tue Sep 15 2026\n\nAt 19Z the forecast area was under southwest flow aloft per the \nlatest water vapor imagery. An upper trough was noted over the \nPacific Northwest while upper ridging remained over the southeastern \nU.S. Surface obs show a frontal boundary bisecting the forecast area.\nNorth of the boundary, cloud cover and occasional rain has kept \ntemps in the 60s and 70s. South of the boundary with good insolation,\ntemps had warmed into the lower and middle 90s. \n\nThe latest NAM and RAP runs continue to show lift over the surface \nboundary increasing this afternoon and persisting through the night. \nA moderately unstable airmass south of the boundary is setting up \nas MUCAPE values increase to between 3000 and 4000 J/kg with bulk \nshear of 30 to 40KT. So there remains a risk for severe storms. \nInverted V soundings and DCAPE near 1000 J/kg support a damaging \nwind risk through this evening along and south of the boundary. Hail \nmay be hard to come by unless there is some strong mid level \nrotation. The RAP analysis suggests freezing levels remain at or \nabove 15 KFT. Models also show very high precipital water values \naround 2.25 inches. This would be near record levels for mid \nSeptember and the reason the forecast anticipates torrential \nrainfall with the storms. With the persistent lift over the boundary \nthrough the night, there could be multiple waves or storms with \nheavy rainfall. Have not made any changes to the flood watch as it \nhighlights the most likely area to see multiple rounds of heavy \nrain. \n\nWith the lift breaking down Wednesday morning, showers and storms \nshould lift to the north and east of the area Wednesday morning. \nThen hot and dry weather is forecast for Thursday and Friday as the \nupper ridge retrogrades over the southern plains and middle MS river \nvalley. This is expected to push precip chances mainly to the north \nof the forecast area while highs in the 90s persist. Will need to \nkeep an eye on the warm air advection pattern setting up Thursday \nmorning for some elevated showers. Some of the short term models are \nshowing some potential while the NBM still has a dry forecast going. \n\nThe operational models continue to show an upper trough moving from \nthe northern Rockies into the plains this weekend. Ensemble data \nstill shows a decent spread in the timing and strength of the \ntrough. But the overall trend is noted in most guidance with chances \nfor showers and storms and cooler temps moving in. The NBM/WPC \nforecast has a broad brush of POPs in the forecast due to the large \nspread and lower predictability. The 12Z ECMWF and GFS seem to \nhighlight Saturday night and Sunday and the window for the front to \nmove through the central plains. This is when the higher POPs are \nforecast to occur.",
